{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788137826,"nonce":"pokGM+AMQtmDLkqNxt09Iwc/ZcA=","message":"SEED LESSONS from Evan — orchestrator session 5, mid-session exchange. Game\nknowledge, not derivable from source; record verbatim-in-substance so 4.1's\nworker starts with them.\n\n1. **Colonists don't put down trees themselves.** Wood (and every raw\n   material) only flows while a designation exists: bills' ingredient-finders\n   haul what is already harvested, nothing more. So material supply is a\n   STANDING designation loop, not a one-off act — checklist entry: \"wood/raw\n   food banked below N days → designate the next chop/growing/mining batch\"\n   (2.4 rollups give the counts; 3.2 is the hand). A bill that stalls with\n   materials at zero should page the checklist, not retry the bill.\n\n2. **When you place a workbench, make sure it's indoors.** Siting policy the\n   API cannot derive: benches (and their product piles) deteriorate unroofed,\n   and room context matters (clean kitchen, cold storage nearby, don't\n   butcher where people eat). The MECHANISMS exist — `room-at` says whether a\n   cell is in a real room, 2.4's roofed/sheltered field flags exposure,\n   3.3's place-layout preflight reports per-cell failures — but the RULE\n   (\"benches go indoors, in the right room\") is playbook material: encode as\n   a siting checklist item and bake into the room templates (the\n   lesson→checklist→template escalation this spec defines).\n\nBoth came from Dorian/Evan review rather than any audit — the exact channel\nDESIGN gives for game knowledge. 4.1's worker: start the checklist with\nthese two.","files":null}]}